(>> Solution to Question 1:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Define elementary row operations on matrices, explain Row Echelon Form \(REF\) and Reduced Row Echelon Form \(RREF\), and prove that elementary operations preserve matrix rank.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Elementary Row Operations \(EROs\):) Tj
T*
(   Let A be an m x n matrix over field F. The three elementary row operations are:) Tj
T*
(   - Type I: Interchanging two rows: Ri <-> Rj.) Tj
T*
(   - Type II: Multiplying a row by a non-zero scalar c in F*: Ri -> c * Ri \(c != 0\).) Tj
T*
(   - Type III: Adding a scalar multiple of one row to another: Ri -> Ri + k * Rj \(i != j, k in F\).) Tj
T*
(   2. Row Echelon Form \(REF\) and Reduced Row Echelon Form \(RREF\):) Tj
T*
(   - A matrix is in REF if:) Tj
T*
(   \(a\) All zero rows \(rows consisting entirely of zeros\) are grouped at the bottom.) Tj
T*
(   \(b\) The first non-zero entry from the left in any non-zero row \(called the pivot or leading) Tj
T*
(   entry\) occurs strictly to the right of the pivot in the row immediately above it.) Tj
T*
(   \(c\) All entries in the column directly below each pivot are zero.) Tj
T*
(   - A matrix is in RREF if in addition to REF:) Tj
T*
(   \(d\) Every pivot entry is equal to 1.) Tj
T*
(   \(e\) Each pivot 1 is the ONLY non-zero entry in its entire column \(entries above and below are) Tj
T*
(   zero\).) Tj
T*
(   3. Proof of Rank Invariance:) Tj
T*
(   - Every elementary row operation corresponds to pre-multiplying matrix A by an elementary) Tj
T*
(   matrix E: A' = E * A.) Tj
T*
(   - Elementary matrices E are invertible \(non-singular\) with det\(E\) != 0:) Tj
T*
(   * For Type I: E^-1 is itself, det\(E\) = -1 != 0.) Tj
T*
(   * For Type II: E^-1 has 1/c on diagonal, det\(E\) = c != 0.) Tj
T*
(   * For Type III: E^-1 replaces k with -k, det\(E\) = 1 != 0.) Tj
T*
(   - The row space of A, Row\(A\), is the subspace of R^n spanned by the row vectors of A.) Tj
T*
(   - Type I simply reorders spanning vectors; Type II scales a spanning vector by a non-zero) Tj
T*
(   constant; Type III replaces a vector r_i with r_i + k*r_j, which belongs to span\(r_i, r_j\).) Tj
T*
(   - Therefore, Row\(A'\) = Row\(A\). Since rank\(A\) = dim\(Row\(A\)\), rank\(A'\) = rank\(A\).) Tj
T*
(   - Hence, elementary row operations strictly preserve matrix rank.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 2:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Determine the rank of the following 4x4 matrix A by systematically reducing it to Row Echelon Form: A = [[1, 2, -1, 3], [2, 4, 1, -2], [3, 6, 3, -7], [1, 2, 2, -5]].) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Step 1: Write down the initial matrix A:) Tj
T*
(   [ 1 2 -1 3 ]) Tj
T*
(   A = [ 2 4 1 -2 ]) Tj
T*
(   [ 3 6 3 -7 ]) Tj
T*
(   [ 1 2 2 -5 ]) Tj
T*
(   Step 2: Use pivot a11 = 1 to eliminate non-zero entries in column 1 below row 1:) Tj
T*
(   Operation R2 -> R2 - 2*R1:) Tj
T*
(   [2 - 2\(1\), 4 - 2\(2\), 1 - 2\(-1\), -2 - 2\(3\)] = [0, 0, 3, -8]) Tj
T*
(   Operation R3 -> R3 - 3*R1:) Tj
T*
(   [3 - 3\(1\), 6 - 3\(2\), 3 - 3\(-1\), -7 - 3\(3\)] = [0, 0, 6, -16]) Tj
T*
(   Operation R4 -> R4 - 1*R1:) Tj
T*
(   [1 - 1\(1\), 2 - 1\(2\), 2 - 1\(-1\), -5 - 1\(3\)] = [0, 0, 3, -8]) Tj
T*
(   Matrix after Step 2:) Tj
T*
(   [ 1 2 -1 3 ]) Tj
T*
(   A1 = [ 0 0 3 -8 ]) Tj
T*
ET

endstream
