(>> Solution to Question 4:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Investigate the consistency of the following system of equations for different values of lambda and mu: x + y + z = 6; x + 2y + 3z = 10; x + 2y + lambda*z = mu. Find the conditions for: \(i\) No solution, \(ii\) A unique solution, \(iii\) Infinitely many solutions.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Step 1: Set up the augmented matrix [A | B]:) Tj
T*
(   [A | B] = [ 1 1 1 | 6 ]) Tj
T*
(   [ 1 2 3 | 10 ]) Tj
T*
(   [ 1 2 lambda | mu ]) Tj
T*
(   Step 2: Perform elementary row operations to reduce [A | B] to echelon form:) Tj
T*
(   Operation R2 -> R2 - R1:) Tj
T*
(   [1 - 1, 2 - 1, 3 - 1 | 10 - 6] = [0, 1, 2 | 4]) Tj
T*
(   Operation R3 -> R3 - R1:) Tj
T*
(   [1 - 1, 2 - 1, lambda - 1 | mu - 6] = [0, 1, lambda - 1 | mu - 6]) Tj
T*
(   Augmented matrix becomes:) Tj
T*
(   [ 1 1 1 | 6 ]) Tj
T*
(   [ 0 1 2 | 4 ]) Tj
T*
(   [ 0 1 lambda - 1 | mu - 6 ]) Tj
T*
(   Step 3: Eliminate entry under second pivot:) Tj
T*
(   Operation R3 -> R3 - R2:) Tj
T*
(   [0 - 0, 1 - 1, \(lambda - 1\) - 2 | \(mu - 6\) - 4] = [0, 0, lambda - 3 | mu - 10]) Tj
T*
(   Final Echelon Form:) Tj
T*
(   [ 1 1 1 | 6 ]) Tj
T*
(   [ 0 1 2 | 4 ]) Tj
T*
(   [ 0 0 lambda - 3 | mu - 10 ]) Tj
T*
(   Step 4: Analyze cases:) Tj
T*
(   Case \(i\): No Solution \(Inconsistent\)) Tj
T*
(   For inconsistency, rank\(A\) must be strictly less than rank\([A | B]\).) Tj
T*
(   This occurs when the third row of A has all zeros while the augmented entry is non-zero:) Tj
T*
(   lambda - 3 = 0 AND mu - 10 != 0) Tj
T*
(   => lambda = 3 and mu != 10.) Tj
T*
(   Here rank\(A\) = 2, rank\([A | B]\) = 3 => No solution.) Tj
T*
(   Case \(ii\): Unique Solution) Tj
T*
(   For a unique solution, rank\(A\) = rank\([A | B]\) = 3 \(number of unknowns n = 3\).) Tj
T*
(   This requires the pivot in row 3 to be non-zero:) Tj
T*
(   lambda - 3 != 0 => lambda != 3 \(for ANY value of mu\).) Tj
T*
(   Here rank\(A\) = rank\([A | B]\) = 3 => Unique solution.) Tj
T*
(   Case \(iii\): Infinitely Many Solutions) Tj
T*
(   For infinitely many solutions, rank\(A\) = rank\([A | B]\) = r < 3.) Tj
T*
(   This requires both lambda - 3 = 0 and mu - 10 = 0:) Tj
T*
(   => lambda = 3 and mu = 10.) Tj
T*
(   Here rank\(A\) = rank\([A | B]\) = 2 < 3, giving \(3 - 2\) = 1 free parameter and infinitely many) Tj
T*
(   solutions.) Tj
T*
