(   [Problem Statement]: Describe the Gauss-Jordan Elimination method for matrix inversion. Using this method, compute the inverse of matrix A = [[1, 2, 3], [2, 5, 3], [1, 0, 8]].) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Methodology of Gauss-Jordan Inversion:) Tj
T*
(   - Construct the augmented partitioned matrix [ A | I_n ], where I_n is the n x n identity) Tj
T*
(   matrix.) Tj
T*
(   - Apply elementary row operations to transform the left half A into the identity matrix I_n:) Tj
T*
(   [ A | I_n ] -> E_k ... E_2 E_1 [ A | I_n ] = [ I_n | A^-1 ].) Tj
T*
(   - The resulting right half is the exact matrix inverse A^-1 because E_k ... E_1 * A = I => E_k) Tj
T*
(   ... E_1 = A^-1.) Tj
T*
(   2. Step-by-Step Computation for A = [[1, 2, 3], [2, 5, 3], [1, 0, 8]]:) Tj
T*
(   Step 1: Setup augmented matrix:) Tj
T*
(   [ 1 2 3 | 1 0 0 ]) Tj
T*
(   [ 2 5 3 | 0 1 0 ]) Tj
T*
(   [ 1 0 8 | 0 0 1 ]) Tj
T*
(   Step 2: Eliminate below a11 = 1:) Tj
T*
(   R2 -> R2 - 2*R1: [ 0 1 -3 | -2 1 0 ]) Tj
T*
(   R3 -> R3 - 1*R1: [ 0 -2 5 | -1 0 1 ]) Tj
T*
ET

