(>> Solution to Question 9:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Define Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors of a linear operator. Prove that eigenvectors corresponding to distinct eigenvalues of any square matrix are linearly independent.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Definitions:) Tj
T*
(   - Let A be an n x n matrix over field F. A scalar lambda in F is called an Eigenvalue \(latent) Tj
T*
(   root\) of A if there exists a non-zero vector X in F^n \(X != 0\) such that:) Tj
T*
(   A * X = lambda * X, or equivalently: \(A - lambda * I\) * X = 0.) Tj
T*
(   - The non-zero vector X is called an Eigenvector corresponding to lambda.) Tj
T*
(   - The set of all vectors satisfying \(A - lambda*I\)X = 0 forms the Eigenspace E_lambda = Null\(A) Tj
T*
(   - lambda*I\).) Tj
T*
(   - Non-trivial solutions X != 0 exist if and only if det\(A - lambda * I\) = 0 \(the Characteristic) Tj
T*
(   Equation\).) Tj
T*
(   2. Proof of Linear Independence:) Tj
T*
(   - Let lambda_1, lambda_2, ..., lambda_k be distinct eigenvalues of A \(lambda_i != lambda_j for) Tj
T*
(   i != j\).) Tj
T*
(   - Let X_1, X_2, ..., X_k be corresponding non-zero eigenvectors such that A * X_i = lambda_i *) Tj
T*
(   X_i.) Tj
T*
(   - We prove linear independence by induction on k:) Tj
T*
(   - Base Case \(k = 1\): Since X1 != 0, c1 * X1 = 0 implies c1 = 0. Thus {X1} is linearly) Tj
T*
(   independent.) Tj
T*
(   - Inductive Step: Assume any set of \(m - 1\) eigenvectors corresponding to distinct eigenvalues) Tj
T*
(   is linearly independent.) Tj
T*
(   Consider a linear combination of m eigenvectors equal to the zero vector:) Tj
T*
(   \(Eq. 1\) c1*X1 + c2*X2 + ... + cm*Xm = 0.) Tj
T*
(   - Multiply Eq. 1 from the left by matrix A:) Tj
T*
(   c1*\(A*X1\) + c2*\(A*X2\) + ... + cm*\(A*Xm\) = 0) Tj
T*
(   => \(Eq. 2\) c1*lambda_1*X1 + c2*lambda_2*X2 + ... + cm*lambda_m*Xm = 0.) Tj
T*
(   - Multiply Eq. 1 by scalar lambda_m:) Tj
T*
(   => \(Eq. 3\) c1*lambda_m*X1 + c2*lambda_m*X2 + ... + cm*lambda_m*Xm = 0.) Tj
T*
(   - Subtract Eq. 3 from Eq. 2:) Tj
T*
(   c1*\(lambda_1 - lambda_m\)*X1 + c2*\(lambda_2 - lambda_m\)*X2 + ... + c_{m-1}*\(lambda_{m-1} -) Tj
T*
(   lambda_m\)*X_{m-1} + 0 = 0.) Tj
T*
(   - By the induction hypothesis, {X1, X2, ..., X_{m-1}} is linearly independent. Hence each) Tj
T*
(   coefficient must be zero:) Tj
T*
(   c_i * \(lambda_i - lambda_m\) = 0 for each i = 1, 2, ..., m - 1.) Tj
T*
(   - Since all eigenvalues are distinct, lambda_i != lambda_m, which means \(lambda_i - lambda_m\)) Tj
T*
(   != 0.) Tj
T*
(   - Therefore: c_i = 0 for all i = 1, 2, ..., m - 1.) Tj
T*
(   - Substituting c1 = c2 = ... = c_{m-1} = 0 back into Eq. 1 gives: cm * Xm = 0.) Tj
T*
(   - Since Xm != 0, we must have cm = 0.) Tj
T*
(   - Thus, all coefficients c1 = c2 = ... = cm = 0.) Tj
T*
(   - Hence, {X1, X2, ..., Xm} is linearly independent. By mathematical induction, the theorem) Tj
T*
(   holds for all k.) Tj
T*
