(   - Let A be an n x n matrix. The characteristic polynomial is defined as:) Tj
T*
(   p\(lambda\) = det\(lambda * I - A\) = lambda^n - c_{n-1}*lambda^{n-1} + ... + \(-1\)^n * c0.) Tj
T*
(   - By definition of determinant, the only term in det\(lambda*I - A\) containing lambda^n and) Tj
T*
(   lambda^{n-1} comes from the product of the diagonal elements:) Tj
T*
(   Product_{i=1}^n \(lambda - a_ii\) = lambda^n - \(sum_{i=1}^n a_ii\) * lambda^{n-1} + ...) Tj
T*
(   - Therefore, the coefficient of lambda^{n-1} is: -sum_{i=1}^n a_ii = -Trace\(A\).) Tj
T*
(   - By the fundamental theorem of algebra, p\(lambda\) factors completely over C into its roots) Tj
T*
(   \(eigenvalues\):) Tj
T*
(   p\(lambda\) = \(lambda - lambda_1\) * \(lambda - lambda_2\) * ... * \(lambda - lambda_n\)) Tj
T*
(   = lambda^n - \(sum_{i=1}^n lambda_i\) * lambda^{n-1} + ... + \(-1\)^n * Product_{i=1}^n lambda_i.) Tj
T*
(   - Equating coefficients of lambda^{n-1}:) Tj
T*
(   -\(sum_{i=1}^n lambda_i\) = -Trace\(A\) => sum_{i=1}^n lambda_i = Trace\(A\). \(Proved\).) Tj
T*
(   2. Proof that Product of Eigenvalues = det\(A\):) Tj
T*
(   - Set lambda = 0 in both expressions of the characteristic polynomial:) Tj
T*
(   From factorization: p\(0\) = \(0 - lambda_1\)\(0 - lambda_2\)...\(0 - lambda_n\) = \(-1\)^n *) Tj
T*
(   Product_{i=1}^n lambda_i.) Tj
T*
(   From determinant definition: p\(0\) = det\(0*I - A\) = det\(-A\) = \(-1\)^n * det\(A\).) Tj
T*
(   - Equating both expressions:) Tj
T*
(   \(-1\)^n * Product_{i=1}^n lambda_i = \(-1\)^n * det\(A\) => Product_{i=1}^n lambda_i = det\(A\).) Tj
T*
(   \(Proved\).) Tj
T*
(   3. Proof for Eigenvalues of A^-1:) Tj
T*
(   - Let A be invertible \(det\(A\) != 0, so lambda != 0\).) Tj
T*
(   - Let X != 0 be an eigenvector corresponding to eigenvalue lambda: A * X = lambda * X.) Tj
T*
(   - Multiply both sides from the left by A^-1:) Tj
T*
(   A^-1 * \(A * X\) = A^-1 * \(lambda * X\)) Tj
T*
(   => I * X = lambda * \(A^-1 * X\)) Tj
T*
(   => X = lambda * \(A^-1 * X\).) Tj
T*
(   - Since lambda != 0, divide both sides by scalar lambda:) Tj
T*
(   A^-1 * X = \(1 / lambda\) * X.) Tj
T*
(   - This matches the eigenvector definition: X is an eigenvector of A^-1 with eigenvalue) Tj
T*
(   1/lambda. \(Proved\).) Tj
T*
