(   - A complex number equals its conjugate if and only if its imaginary part is zero.) Tj
T*
(   - Hence, lambda is strictly REAL. \(Proved\).) Tj
T*
(   2. Proof of Orthogonality of Eigenvectors:) Tj
T*
(   - Let lambda_1 and lambda_2 be two distinct eigenvalues \(lambda_1 != lambda_2\) of real) Tj
T*
(   symmetric matrix A.) Tj
T*
(   - Let X_1 and X_2 be corresponding real eigenvectors:) Tj
T*
(   A * X_1 = lambda_1 * X_1 and A * X_2 = lambda_2 * X_2.) Tj
T*
(   - Consider the scalar quantity X_1^T * A * X_2:) Tj
T*
(   X_1^T * \(A * X_2\) = X_1^T * \(lambda_2 * X_2\) = lambda_2 * \(X_1^T * X_2\).) Tj
T*
(   - Also, using the transpose property \(A * X_1\)^T = X_1^T * A^T = X_1^T * A:) Tj
T*
(   \(X_1^T * A\) * X_2 = \(A * X_1\)^T * X_2 = \(lambda_1 * X_1\)^T * X_2 = lambda_1 * \(X_1^T * X_2\).) Tj
T*
(   - Subtracting the two expressions:) Tj
T*
(   lambda_1 * \(X_1^T * X_2\) - lambda_2 * \(X_1^T * X_2\) = 0) Tj
T*
(   => \(lambda_1 - lambda_2\) * \(X_1^T * X_2\) = 0.) Tj
T*
(   - Since lambda_1 != lambda_2, we have \(lambda_1 - lambda_2\) != 0.) Tj
T*
(   - Consequently: X_1^T * X_2 = 0.) Tj
T*
(   - This signifies that the Euclidean inner product <X1, X2> = 0.) Tj
T*
(   - Hence, eigenvectors X_1 and X_2 are mutually ORTHOGONAL. \(Proved\).) Tj
T*
