(>> Solution to Question 13:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: State the Cayley-Hamilton Theorem. Provide a rigorous algebraic proof for an arbitrary 2x2 matrix, and explain its general proof for an n x n matrix using the adjoint matrix identity.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Statement of Cayley-Hamilton Theorem:) Tj
T*
(   Every square matrix A of order n satisfies its own characteristic equation.) Tj
T*
(   That is, if the characteristic polynomial of A is:) Tj
T*
(   p\(lambda\) = det\(lambda * I - A\) = lambda^n + c_{n-1}*lambda^{n-1} + ... + c1*lambda + c0 = 0,) Tj
T*
(   then substituting the matrix A for scalar lambda and c0*I for constant c0 yields the zero) Tj
T*
(   matrix:) Tj
T*
(   p\(A\) = A^n + c_{n-1}*A^{n-1} + ... + c1*A + c0*I = O.) Tj
T*
(   2. Direct Proof for a General 2x2 Matrix:) Tj
T*
(   - Let A = [a, b; c, d].) Tj
T*
(   - The characteristic equation is det\(lambda*I - A\) = 0:) Tj
T*
(   det\([lambda - a, -b; -c, lambda - d]\) = \(lambda - a\)\(lambda - d\) - bc) Tj
T*
(   = lambda^2 - \(a + d\)*lambda + \(ad - bc\) = 0.) Tj
T*
(   - We must verify that: A^2 - \(a + d\)*A + \(ad - bc\)*I = O.) Tj
T*
(   - Compute A^2:) Tj
T*
(   A^2 = [a, b; c, d] * [a, b; c, d] = [a^2 + bc, ab + bd; ac + cd, bc + d^2].) Tj
T*
(   - Compute \(a + d\)*A:) Tj
T*
(   \(a + d\)*A = [\(a+d\)a, \(a+d\)b; \(a+d\)c, \(a+d\)d] = [a^2 + ad, ab + bd; ac + cd, ad + d^2].) Tj
T*
(   - Subtract:) Tj
T*
(   A^2 - \(a + d\)*A = [ \(a^2+bc\) - \(a^2+ad\), \(ab+bd\) - \(ab+bd\) ]) Tj
T*
(   [ \(ac+cd\) - \(ac+cd\), \(bc+d^2\) - \(ad+d^2\) ]) Tj
T*
(   = [ bc - ad, 0 ]) Tj
T*
(   [ 0, bc - ad ] = -\(ad - bc\) * [1, 0; 0, 1] = -\(ad - bc\)*I.) Tj
T*
(   - Adding \(ad - bc\)*I to both sides yields:) Tj
T*
(   A^2 - \(a + d\)*A + \(ad - bc\)*I = [0, 0; 0, 0] = O. Hence proved for 2x2.) Tj
T*
(   3. General Proof for n x n Matrix using Adjoint Identity:) Tj
T*
(   - For any matrix M, M * adj\(M\) = det\(M\) * I.) Tj
T*
(   - Let M = \(lambda * I - A\). Then:) Tj
T*
(   \(lambda * I - A\) * adj\(lambda * I - A\) = det\(lambda * I - A\) * I = p\(lambda\) * I.) Tj
T*
(   - Since entries of adj\(lambda*I - A\) are cofactors of order \(n - 1\), they are polynomials in) Tj
T*
(   lambda of degree at most n - 1:) Tj
T*
(   adj\(lambda * I - A\) = B_{n-1} * lambda^{n-1} + B_{n-2} * lambda^{n-2} + ... + B1 * lambda + B0,) Tj
T*
(   where each B_k is an n x n constant matrix.) Tj
T*
(   - Equating coefficients of powers of lambda:) Tj
T*
(   lambda^n: B_{n-1} = I) Tj
T*
(   lambda^{n-1}: B_{n-2} - A * B_{n-1} = c_{n-1} * I) Tj
T*
(   ...) Tj
T*
(   lambda^1: B0 - A * B1 = c1 * I) Tj
T*
(   lambda^0: -A * B0 = c0 * I.) Tj
T*
(   - Multiply the equation for lambda^k from the left by A^k:) Tj
T*
ET

