(   [Problem Statement]: Explain LU Decomposition \(Doolittle factorization\) of a square matrix. Factorize matrix A = [[2, 1, 1], [4, 1, 0], [-2, 2, 1]] into L and U, and solve AX = [1, -2, 7]^T.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Methodology of Doolittle LU Decomposition:) Tj
T*
(   - Any square matrix A can be factored as A = L * U \(provided no row interchanges are required\),) Tj
T*
(   where:) Tj
T*
(   * L is a unit lower triangular matrix \(1s on main diagonal\): l_ii = 1, l_ij = 0 for j > i.) Tj
T*
(   * U is an upper triangular matrix: u_ij = 0 for i > j.) Tj
T*
(   - Solving AX = B proceeds in two fast forward/backward substitution steps:) Tj
T*
(   Step 1: Set UX = Y, then A*X = L*\(U*X\) = L*Y = B. Solve L*Y = B by Forward Substitution.) Tj
T*
(   Step 2: Solve U*X = Y by Backward Substitution.) Tj
T*
(   2. Step-by-Step LU Factorization of A:) Tj
T*
(   Let A = [ 2 1 1; 4 1 0; -2 2 1 ].) Tj
T*
(   - Row 1 of U:) Tj
T*
(   u11 = a11 = 2; u12 = a12 = 1; u13 = a13 = 1.) Tj
T*
(   - Col 1 of L:) Tj
T*
(   l21 = a21 / u11 = 4 / 2 = 2.) Tj
T*
(   l31 = a31 / u11 = -2 / 2 = -1.) Tj
T*
(   - Row 2 of U:) Tj
T*
